+### Moving long foreground commands to the background
+
+By default a foreground command runs until it exits or reaches its timeout,
+and a command that times out is killed. Set `terminal.auto_background_seconds`
+to move a foreground command that is still running after that many seconds to
+the background instead, the same as if it had been started with
+`background=true` and `notify=true`. The command is not killed or restarted.
+The tool result has `status: "auto_backgrounded"`, the `session_id`, the output
+so far, and a note that tells the agent the command is still running, that it
+will be notified when the command exits, and that it must not run it again.
+The completion notice (exit code and output tail) arrives like any other
+`notify_on_complete` completion.
+
+```yaml
+terminal:
+  auto_background_seconds: 120   # 0 (default) = off
+```
+
+This applies to the local backend, and only where a completion can reach the
+agent (the same sessions as the repeated-wait rule below). Elsewhere, and on
+backends that cannot hand a running command over, the command keeps today's
+behavior and runs in the foreground until it exits or times out. A value at or
+above the command's timeout has no effect.
+
 ### Repeated waits on a quiet process
 
 When the agent calls `process(action="wait")` or `process(action="poll")` twice in a row on a
